    "title": "AssumeUTXO: crowdsource trust through independent snapshot verification",
    "body": "## Problem\n\nWith AssumeUTXO, a node starts from a UTXO snapshot in an effectively pruned state and validates new blocks immediately, while downloading and validating earlier blocks in the background to become an archival node.\nThis leaves us in a confusing limbo: we trust the expected UTXO set hash enough to use the snapshot, but still treat the blocks preceding it as unvalidated until background validation finishes.\nIf we trust the hash, why does every snapshot user have to validate those blocks? If we do not, what assurance are users relying on in the meantime?\nIf background validation finds that the reconstructed UTXO set does not match the snapshot, it is unclear what users should do.\n\nManaging multiple chainstates adds substantial maintenance work across [RPC](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/33259) and [wallet reporting](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/28616), [notifications](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/27596#discussion_r1291620032), [pruning](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/27596#discussion_r1311007889), [cache allocation](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35744), [indexes](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/27596#discussion_r1343273598), and [reorgs](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/34535), and introduces [race conditions](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35465#discussion_r3428902672) and [unintuitive corner cases](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35307).\nScript-check logs need to [distinguish snapshot and background validation](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/32975#discussion_r2264099546), [restarts need extra completion checks](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/issues/32029) (even for a fully validated snapshot), and RPCs report [different progress for the same background chainstate](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36220).\nReviewing even an ordinary validation fix requires reasoning about [block candidates in both chainstates](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/34521#pullrequestreview-3761920357), making the most critical part of the codebase harder to understand and review.\n\nAssumeUTXO shortens the wait for a usable node, but completing background validation still requires [downloading the snapshot in addition to all the blocks](https://groups.google.com/g/bitcoindev/c/rThmyI8ZN3Q/m/gHeRYMhTCAAJ) and temporarily keeping [two UTXO databases](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/master/doc/assumeutxo.md#pruning).\nEach chainstate also starts its own [prevout-fetching worker pool](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35738#discussion_r3599383063) when enabled, doubling the number of workers.\nThe [cache budget follows a fixed 95/5 split](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/aef8a04966d7ab6c04edc5d19b733e78205405de/src/validation.cpp#L6103-L6132) that reverses once the snapshot chainstate catches up, then goes entirely to that chainstate when background validation finishes.\nThese changing budgets complicate presizing the UTXO cache, and resizing can require flushing and discarding cached entries or reopening the database.\nAssumeUTXO [adoption remains below expectations](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35054#issuecomment-4468360424).\n\n[@ryanofsky raised similar concerns about code complexity in the original implementation PR](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/15606#issuecomment-869986387), arguing that validation outside `ChainstateManager` should not need to know about the different AssumeUTXO phases and roles.\n[David Harding's original feedback](https://github.com/jamesob/assumeutxo-docs/blob/2019-04-proposal/proposal/feedback-david-harding.txt) also questioned keeping old snapshots and requiring every snapshot user to validate earlier blocks, while calling for easy hash checks by ordinary nodes.\n\nWe already bake the expected UTXO set hash at the snapshot block into the source code and binary, but normal IBD ignores it completely.\n\n## Proposed approach\n\nNodes doing a full IBD could use the baked-in hash to check their own UTXO set, giving others confidence to bootstrap more quickly from the snapshot.\nThese routine checks could replace background validation on snapshot nodes, letting them skip the blocks before the snapshot and run validation on a single chainstate.\n\n### 1. Check the UTXO set hash during IBD\n\nWhen a node doing IBD reaches the snapshot height, it could hash the flushed UTXO set and compare the result with the hash in Core.\nWith this check enabled by default, every full IBD would strengthen trust in the hash, much like [reproducible builds](https://github.com/bitcoin-core/guix.sigs#readme) do for release binaries (we don't have a background-recompilation either): a single honest participant is enough to expose a mismatch for everyone to investigate.\n\n### 2. Keep only the latest UTXO set hash\n\nKeeping only the latest UTXO snapshot hash would let nodes running the same release share one reference and scan the UTXO set only once during IBD.\nOlder snapshots (including those whose background validation is unfinished) would remain usable with a compatible older release.\n\n### 3. Make UTXO snapshot trust an explicit choice\n\nUsers already choose how much to verify during IBD: `-assumevalid=0` checks every script, while the default can skip scripts in older blocks.\nRelying on the independent checks above would extend that choice to snapshot startup, removing the chainstate role changes, cache splitting, and special handling of progress reporting and block downloads.\n\nWithout background validation, a snapshot node would lack the earlier blocks, so wallet rescans, indexes, and serving old blocks could follow the existing pruning behavior.\nWe could also [improve wallet and index support for pruning](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/issues/36476), so they handle missing blocks more reliably.\nUsers who later want an unpruned node could disable pruning and run with `-reindex` to download and validate all blocks, rebuilding the UTXO set from scratch.\nSince the snapshot would be trusted from the outset, restoring the missing blocks would be optional rather than a required step before trusting the node.\n\n## Why the IBD check is not a new checkpoint\n\nThe IBD check would compare against a hash that already exists, without changing the rules for accepting blocks or choosing the valid chain with the most work.\nThe node would warn if its UTXO set did not match the baked-in hash, without stopping IBD.\nAn extreme reorg could replace the snapshot block itself, in which case the node would report that change and skip the comparison, since the baked-in hash describes a different chain.\n\n## Cost and related work\n\nChecking the UTXO set takes [a few minutes](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/issues/32029#issuecomment-2715987586), and the check could be disabled or moved to a separate thread if needed.\n\nThe check could also build confidence in faster approaches such as [pruneassumevalid](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/issues/36476) and [SwiftSync](https://github.com/bitcoin/bips/pull/2152) by confirming that they produce the expected UTXO set.\n\n[P2P UTXO snapshot distribution](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35054) could make AssumeUTXO easier to use and to integrate into node packages, and the single-chainstate approach proposed here could address [concerns about extending multiple-chainstate validation](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35054#issuecomment-4488453829).\nThe discussion already considered [removing background validation to save bandwidth and reduce complexity](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35054#issuecomment-4501781606), and users who [want to validate every block locally](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/35054#issuecomment-4530515268) could do so afterward with `-reindex`.\n",
